WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.569
"Concretely, NeuPL trains one opponent-conditioned
network.

00:00:03.649 --> 00:00:08.270
Each policy index i is paired with a meta-strategy
vector sigma-i that says,

00:00:08.350 --> 00:00:12.908
as a probability distribution, which other policies
it should play against.

00:00:12.988 --> 00:00:16.991
The network maximizes discounted return under a
double expectation

00:00:17.071 --> 00:00:18.970
first over the sampled opponent,

00:00:19.050 --> 00:00:22.248
then over the game dynamics induced by both policies.

00:00:22.328 --> 00:00:24.907
To optimize this by reinforcement learning,

00:00:24.987 --> 00:00:29.051
NeuPL jointly trains an opponent-conditioned
action-value function,

00:00:29.131 --> 00:00:34.680
and additionally learns a payoff estimator that
predicts the expected outcome of any policy

00:00:34.760 --> 00:00:37.215
pairing directly from the value function,

00:00:37.295 --> 00:00:42.349
so the full payoff matrix can be evaluated cheaply
without replaying every matchup.

00:00:42.429 --> 00:00:46.431
The interaction graph that assigns opponents can be
a fixed matrix

00:00:46.511 --> 00:00:48.905
reproducing fictitious play or adaptive,

00:00:48.985 --> 00:00:53.977
recomputed from the learned payoffs by a meta-graph
solver, reproducing PSRO-Nash.

00:00:54.057 --> 00:00:59.172
Because the graph, not a handcrafted schedule,
controls which objectives are active,

00:00:59.252 --> 00:01:04.120
the number of genuinely distinct policies grows on
its own as the game demands."
